Description: Put applications into their systemd scope before they start.
 GNOME Shell, gnome-session and gnome-settings-daemon ask systemd to move each application they launch with
 GDesktopAppInfo into a scope of its own, app-gnome-<application ID>-<pid>.scope, but only once GLib has started it.
 systemd moves only the process it is given, so every process the application started in the meantime stays in the
 cgroup of the service which launched it. Chromium and Electron applications start their zygotes and their GPU process
 at once, which then stay in GNOME Shell's or gnome-session's cgroup, are stopped with it before the application at
 logout, and the application crashes when they go.
 .
 GLib asks systemd for the scope itself instead, right after it spawned gio-launch-desktop, and gio-launch-desktop
 waits until systemd has moved it into the scope before it executes the application, which therefore starts every
 process of its own in the scope. The launching process sends the request before the launch call returns, and follows
 it to its end: when systemd refused it, or the job starting the scope ended with another result than done, it writes
 the reason to a pipe to gio-launch-desktop, which then runs the application at once. Otherwise gio-launch-desktop
 waits as long as systemd needs, so that a busy systemd delays the application instead of splitting it.
 gio-launch-desktop gives up, after 60 seconds, only when nothing can tell it the outcome: when the launching process
 exited before systemd was done, or never runs its main loop again.
 .
 The scope is requested only by processes managed by the systemd user instance, outside of sandboxes, and only when
 systemd is on the session bus, which the bus answers itself, and the name is short enough for systemd, as those
 refusals are certain. The process is given to systemd as a pidfd, when the bus can pass one, as systemd would look up
 a process ID in its own PID namespace, which is not the one of a launching process in a sandbox of its own which uses
 the session bus. The process counts as in the scope also in a cgroup below the scope's.
 .
 gio-launch-desktop logs at error priority when the application runs outside of its scope after all, with the reason
 the launching process passed on, or after the 60 seconds. Neither is expected. A refusal because systemd is stopping,
 at the end of a session, is logged at notice priority. When standard error is the journal, whose stream gives every
 line the same priority, the message is sent through syslog, so that it keeps its priority.
 .
 The scope has the name gnome-desktop's gnome_start_systemd_scope() gives it, so that a launching process which still
 asks for it afterwards fails without moving any process, and gnome-session's drop-in for app-gnome-*.scope applies.
 The "launched" signal names the scope as "systemd-unit" in its platform data, so that launching processes can leave
 the scope to GLib.
Author: Mitar
Bug: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/glib/-/merge_requests/1596
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-06

